    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_session_looks_stable_Title">No obvious bottleneck found</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_session_looks_stable_Body">Keep this session as a baseline and compare future runs against it after app updates or settings changes.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_crash_like_exit_Title">Treat this run as a crash investigation</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_crash_like_exit_Body">Re-run the same target with child processes enabled, then compare the new session. If the exit repeats with similar timing, collect the exported report and check the target app logs around the recorded exit time.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_crash_like_exit_Evidence">A crash-like exit was recorded. Details and exit code are preserved in the session report.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_unexpected_exit_Title">Verify why the target closed</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_unexpected_exit_Body">If the close was not intentional, repeat the run and watch the last event before exit. A matching final CPU, RAM, or disk spike is a stronger lead than the exit signal alone.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_unexpected_exit_Evidence">The target exited without a normal stop signal.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_hang_suspected_Title">Investigate responsiveness, not only resource usage</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_hang_suspected_Body">Check the event context for nearby top CPU, RAM, and disk processes. If resource usage is low during the hang, the target may be blocked on UI, I/O, network, or a child process instead of raw CPU pressure.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_hang_suspected_Evidence">The session recorded one or more responsiveness events.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_long_startup_Title">Separate startup cost from runtime cost</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_long_startup_Body">Record one startup-focused run and one already-running attach run. If only startup is slow, focus on launch-time plug-ins, disk reads, updates, and first-load caches.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_long_startup_Evidence">Startup readiness took longer than expected.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_cpu_pressure_Title">CPU is the first suspect</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_cpu_pressure_Body">Repeat with child processes enabled and compare the CPU average. If the child-process run is much higher, the slowdown is probably in a helper process rather than the main executable.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_cpu_pressure_Evidence">Average or peak CPU was high for the selected profile.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_memory_pressure_Title">RAM usage deserves attention</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_memory_pressure_Body">Check whether the target is expected to hold this much memory. For browser, game, or launcher targets, repeat with child processes enabled before blaming the root process.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_memory_pressure_Evidence">Memory reached a high value or climbed during the run.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_disk_read_pressure_Title">Disk reads are a likely source of stalls</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_disk_read_pressure_Body">If this happens near startup or loading screens, compare a second run after caches are warm. If reads stay high, check antivirus scans, game asset loading, or a slow drive.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_disk_read_pressure_Evidence">Disk reads produced spikes or profile-limit breaches.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_disk_write_pressure_Title">Disk writes may be causing stutter</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_disk_write_pressure_Body">Check whether the target is writing logs, shader caches, recordings, or temp files. If writes align with spikes, move captures/cache to a faster drive or reduce write-heavy options.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_disk_write_pressure_Evidence">Disk writes produced spikes or profile-limit breaches.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_enable_child_processes_Title">Repeat once with child processes enabled</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_enable_child_processes_Body">Many launchers, browsers, and games move real work into helper processes. A child-process run makes CPU/RAM/Disk attribution much more trustworthy.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_enable_child_processes_Evidence">This session tracked only the root process.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_short_session_Title">This run is too short for confident trends</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_short_session_Body">Use this session for exit/startup clues only. For CPU, RAM, and disk trends, record at least 30-60 seconds under the workload you care about.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Ui_SessionRec_short_session_Evidence">Too few samples were recorded for a confident trend.</system:String>
